  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=-3$ is a sink\n\n- $y=0$ is a node\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?",
  "worked_solution": "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{\u2032}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $2$ equilibrium points, $y=-3$ and $y=0.$\n\n- We're given that $y=-3$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=-3$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=0$ is a node. This means that the arrows on either side of $y=0$ point in the same direction. Since the arrow just below $y=0$ points downward, the arrow just above it points downward too.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/db7e9ea076c3a1fe2cf5341b841693e36d5b9a9030e624da88d2cfd3b0d3a1b0.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069812/assets/e440928dfa83e3e653a2e508e9d01e53c73df84530402a393e82f56996eb5fde.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-3$ and $y=0.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-3$ appear to converge toward this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=0$ appear to diverge away from this line when we're looking below the line and appear to converge toward the line when we're looking above the line.",
